# Argon OS — base system
# Kept deliberately minimal: every package here must justify itself.
# The kernel is selected by live-build itself (--linux-flavours in auto/config).

## Core system
systemd
# systemd-resolved is a separate package now; Argon's DNS-over-TLS setup
# (etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d) depends on it being present and enabled.
systemd-resolved
sudo
apt
apt-transport-https
gnupg
ca-certificates
locales
console-setup
keyboard-configuration
zstd

## Live boot
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
plymouth
plymouth-themes
# label.so — renders ALL text in script-based plymouth themes (Image.Text).
# It is a separate package and only a Recommends, and recommends are
# globally off. Without it Argon's theme still shows the logo but every
# text element renders as nothing — including the LUKS passphrase prompt,
# so an encrypted install boots into an invisible password prompt and
# looks exactly like a hung/failed boot. fonts-dejavu-core provides the
# font the plymouth initramfs hook copies in for label.so to use.
plymouth-label
fonts-dejavu-core

## Firmware (non-free-firmware area; needed for real hardware support)
firmware-linux-free
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-iwlwifi

## Networking
network-manager
# wpasupplicant is what actually associates with a Wi-Fi network (the WPA/
# WPA2/WPA3 handshake). NetworkManager does not do this itself — it drives
# wpa_supplicant over D-Bus, and with no supplicant present it marks every
# Wi-Fi device "unavailable" and nm-applet reports "device not ready".
#
# It is only a *Recommends* of network-manager, and Argon builds with
# --apt-recommends false (see build/auto/config), so it was silently absent
# from every image: the iwlwifi driver loaded, the firmware loaded, wlan0
# appeared, dmesg was clean — and Wi-Fi was still completely unusable, with
# nothing anywhere saying why. Never rely on the recommends here.
# 0200-argon-services asserts these are present at build time.
wpasupplicant
# The rest of the wireless userspace, stripped by recommends-off for the same
# reason. iw and rfkill are how anyone diagnoses a radio problem (both were
# "command not found" on a real install); wireless-regdb carries the
# regulatory rules the kernel needs to enable channels at legal power.
iw
rfkill
wireless-regdb
# NetworkManager's connection sharing / Wi-Fi hotspot mode runs a dnsmasq
# instance; another recommends-off casualty.
dnsmasq-base
wireguard-tools
openvpn

## Security hardening (installed AND enabled by default — see hooks/)
apparmor
apparmor-profiles
apparmor-utils
ufw
fail2ban
unattended-upgrades
apt-listchanges
needrestart

## Privacy
tor
torsocks
mat2
chrony

## Secure remote access (installed but disabled by default — see hooks/)
openssh-client
openssh-server

## Base utilities
bash-completion
curl
wget
git
vim
nano
htop
less
man-db
rsync
unzip
p7zip-full
cryptsetup
# Unlocks the encrypted root from the initramfs at boot. It is only a
# Recommends of cryptsetup (and recommends are globally off), so without
# listing it an encrypted install would finish fine and then never boot.
cryptsetup-initramfs
btrfs-progs
dosfstools
gparted

## Btrfs snapshots + rollback (only active on btrfs installs; a first-boot
## service configures snapper, and an apt hook snapshots before every
## package transaction so a bad upgrade can be rolled back in one step).
## grub-btrfs (which adds the snapshots to the boot menu) is NOT installed
## here: its postinst runs grub-probe/grub-mkconfig, which cannot work in
## the live-build chroot (no real root device). It belongs on the installed
## system, so the first-boot snapshot setup installs it there instead.
## inotify-tools is its watcher's dependency, staged now so that install is
## quick/offline-friendlier.
snapper
inotify-tools

## Secure Boot tooling (staging). The default boot chain is unchanged and
## unsigned (Kali's kernel isn't Microsoft-signed); these are just the tools
## the opt-in `argon-secureboot-setup` needs to enrol a Machine Owner Key
## and sign the kernel. Nothing here signs or changes anything by itself.
mokutil
sbsigntool
